[Music]  hi there welcome to chess openings  explained to bridged  i'm nick risco and uh with the help of  dennis sporos uh  prep we're going to be going over the  scandinavian defense today we're going  to be going over the queen a5  line it is one of the oldest lines in  chess one of the earliest recorded  back in the 1400s and it'll be a great  look at  how to develop your pieces in a really  open position so the position starts off  with e4 and d5  this is the scandinavian defense and  black is  willing to trade off a center pawn get  his queen out  get a little bit of queen activity and  play a really solid structure  playing moves like c6 and e6 later in  the position  here white takes on d5  and here black has two options either  the queen can take on d5  or the knight can go to f6 uh  and now the one that's coming up  popularity is the knight f6 line  but the original line is queen takes d5  white plays knight c3 getting a tempo on  the queen  developing a piece and here's the line  we're going to be going over  is queen a5 the other options are queen  d6 and queen d8  with queen d8 being more popular at the  club level and  queen d6 becoming a bit more trendy at  the top level  after queen a5 white decides to take  full center control with d4  put a pawn in the center and try and  build around that  black plays knight f6 developing one of  his minor pieces  and white will continue developing  naturally he plays knight f3  getting his other knight out and here  black plays  c6 kind of the pawn structure that i  mentioned earlier  black is going to be following up with  uh getting his bishop outside the pawn  chain before he plays e6  and then this dark square bishop is  going to be trying to come out  a little bit later we're not exactly  sure where it wants to go yet  here white plays bishop c4 already  prepared to castle king side and black  is going to play  bishop f5 just developing the bishop  getting it outside the pawn chain  and here white plays bishop d2  threatening a couple things here  uh mainly this discovery on the queen  after the knight  goes to let's say d5 or even e4  we have a discovery on the queen  so black will play e6 preparing to  get his dark square bishop uh developed  maybe castle kingside  and he already has a good square for his  knight going to d7 to castle queen side  if he needs to  and here white plays either knight d5  or 94 they both transpose but  the cuter move is knight d5 being  attacked  four times and defended once  but because of this discovery on the  queen the knight is completely safe here  black's only move is queen to d8  this is four says it's the only square  that white does not control  and then here white is going to capture  on f6  now queen takes and pawn takes are both  playable but at the top level you'll see  more  players play g takes the reason is  because after a move like  queen takes f6 there's a white gets  really rapid development can get some  tempo on the queen  now say queen e2 knight d7 and we'll  keep developing naturally  and here black's queen is already  attacked and the engine will give this  as a winning position for white with  plus 1.2  so you'll see a lot of top level players  play pawn takes  and here when we look at the structure  we can notice a couple things  one black is not going to castle king's  side but this damaged pawn structure  is not going to be safe to castle and  put the king on this open  file so black is going to be trying to  castle queen side  in order to do this he's going to have  to develop the queen move the knight and  castle queen side which gives white a  little bit of  time to keep developing his pieces  whites will normally go bishop to b3  he's going to be trying to play queen e2  but he doesn't want to let the c  pawn hang to this bishop on f5  after bishop b3 black is going to  continue with his plan of developing  castle and queen side so knight to d7  queen to e2 and then queen to c7  preparing the castle queen side  both sides castle long and here white  will go  knight h4 i should mention white also  has the option to play knight h4  before castling but it will transpose  into the same line where  after knight h4 bishop g7  both sides will castle and this is the  same position  so bishop goes back to g6 if white ends  up capturing on g6 they have the option  to either take with the h  pawn and open up the file for the rook  or capturing with the f  pawn and then undoubling his pawns  getting a more flexible pawn structure  here the most common move played is g3  kind of getting a pawn chain set up may  play f4  may play h4 after this knight moves he  doesn't want to trade and get the bishop  pair just yet because he will have that  option  at least for the next couple moves  black is going to develop their last  piece with bishop to d6  and now this is where white is going to  capture on g6  and black will capture with the h pawn  opening up the file for his rook without  even  moving his rook at all his rook is well  placed on a semi-open file  here white just plays h4 getting this  pawn chain  set up and now this backwards h pawn  on h2 is not going to be a weakness for  white  as it is protected by a pawn  and here black will play f5 just kind of  playing slowly playing solidly and a way  that white could continue  is king b1 and his chess from here  this is the main line of the queen a5  scandinavian  i just want to give another thanks to  grandmaster dennis boros for helping me  prep this  leave your comments down below if  there's any questions we'll try and  answer them thanks for watching

